// Test: bugfixes/codegen_stack_remaining_static_call_to_sibling_caller
// Grammar: static_method_call → ClassName.method(args)  (grammar.ebnf §5
//   static_method_call) on a method whose body has a sibling method call.
// Regression: CODEGEN_STACK_REMAINING (dashboard fp df5b8c9b1021).
//   Reported against compiler 0.30.268. Wrapping bare functions: blocks in
//   stateless `class CapabilityName` and calling them statically (Law 2
//   pattern) produced "type mismatch: expected i32 but nothing on stack".
//   Root cause: the typechecker's auto-static heuristic for utility classes
//   (no parent, no fields) called body_uses_this(), which conservatively
//   returned true for any method whose body contained a sibling method
//   call — because sibling calls lower to MethodCall { receiver: this, ... }.
//   The "caller" method was therefore left as is_static = false, gaining an
//   implicit `this` parameter in the WASM signature. But the user invoked
//   it via static syntax ClassName.method(args), which uses the
//   StaticMethodCall codegen path — that path pushes only the user-visible
//   args, not `this`. The call instruction's arity mismatch surfaced as
//   the WASM validator complaining about a missing stack value.
//
//   Fix: for utility classes (no parent, no fields), mark every method as
//   is_static unconditionally. A class with no state has nothing for `this`
//   to point at, and the only legitimate use of `this` inside such a class
//   is the implicit receiver of sibling method calls — which the MIR
//   builder already drops at the call site when the callee is static.
//
// Expected output:
//   19
//   11
//   42

start:
	integer r = C1.entry(2, 3)
	print(r.toString())
	integer s = C1.compute(2, 3)
	print(s.toString())
	integer t = C2.passthrough(42)
	print(t.toString())

class C1
	functions:
		integer entry(integer a, integer b)
			integer x = compute(a, b)
			return x + 8
		integer compute(integer a, integer b)
			return a + b + 6

class C2
	functions:
		integer passthrough(integer n)
			return n
